findings.claim:
Much of §4 is abstract content that another roadmap already plans. That content is a rank-two representation over the total quotient ring of a reduced local Hecke algebra whose residue field E has characteristic 0, with these steps:
- characteristic polynomials lie in the ring and reduce to (x − 1)(x − χ(σ)), by Chebotarev and continuity;
- at τ with χ(τ) ≠ 1, Hensel gives an adapted basis, with a ≡ 1 and d ≡ χ;
- the module B is generated by the b(σ), with a class κ in H¹(G_F, B̄(χ^{-1}));
- local conditions come from the change of basis to the ordinary triangular basis at p.
IntegralHeckeAndGaloisDeterminants plans this: IHG.1 (generalized matrix algebras, reducibility ideals, extension modules), IHG.4 (uniqueness from Frobenius density by Chebotarev and continuity) and IHG.6 (the integral generalized Ribet theorem with local conditions). The review saw this. Its notes on global-basis, cocycle-kappa and chebotarev-integrality say to import IHG and keep only the specialization, and one says 'the route brief does not list IHG as an import'. But it did not change the brief, which is the design job's only instruction, and IntegralHeckeAndGaloisDeterminants appears nowhere in it. The same machinery is also routed from the Brumer–Stark side. Stage I.7 tells its consumer to import the shared theorem from IHG.6 and not to build 'a second generic construction here'. The accepted PAPER-DASGUPTA-KAKDE-23 plans its Hilbert-modular specialization at I.7 and IHG.6. As briefed, the Gross–Stark Part II would plan the generic Ribet construction again.
